1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
It's a discovery as old as fire and the wheel.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Born of ancient alchemies, it provides shelter, protection, and warmth.

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Durable, pliable, and organic.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
It is always about envy and fascination.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Now, leather on modern marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,640
Near the dawn of civilization, a solitary man began a journey here in the rugged terrain of the Swiss Alps.

7
00:00:45,020 --> 00:00:50,560
Whatever his destination, he never made it, dead from an arrow wound in his torso.

8
00:00:52,200 --> 00:00:58,680
Remarkably, for the next 5,000 years, his mortal remains stayed beautifully preserved by the frigid cold,

9
00:00:58,681 --> 00:01:01,300
until they were discovered in 1991.

10
00:01:02,440 --> 00:01:04,380
But not only was his body intact,

11
00:01:05,160 --> 00:01:06,680
so too were bits of his clothing,

12
00:01:07,520 --> 00:01:09,040
a satchel filled with tools,

13
00:01:09,540 --> 00:01:11,280
and a pair of waterproof boots,

14
00:01:11,780 --> 00:01:13,000
all made of ancient leather.

15
00:01:14,340 --> 00:01:16,700
Leather making goes back to the origins of man

16
00:01:16,701 --> 00:01:20,300
for clothing, for protection, for hunting.

17
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:23,620
We definitely would not have made it through the ice ages

18
00:01:23,621 --> 00:01:25,700
without the ability to tan hide.

19
00:01:26,040 --> 00:01:28,340
We had to be able to figure out how to clothe ourselves

20
00:01:28,341 --> 00:01:30,460
and leather's essential for that.

21
00:01:31,340 --> 00:01:34,000
Born of our ingenuity and will to survive,

22
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,040
leather may well have been the first man-made product.

23
00:01:37,760 --> 00:01:40,360
Its warmth and protections so vital to ancient peoples

24
00:01:40,361 --> 00:01:41,520
that it became sacred.

25
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:45,360
Today, leather is our daily companion.

26
00:01:46,380 --> 00:01:47,680
Nearly all of us walk in it,

27
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:49,620
ride on it,

28
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:51,840
and wrap it around ourselves.

29
00:01:53,020 --> 00:01:56,140
Yet the essence of the material remains the same as it was

30
00:01:56,141 --> 00:01:57,420
at the dawn of civilization.

31
00:01:57,421 --> 00:01:59,420
raw animal skins.

32
00:02:00,120 --> 00:02:02,620
That is what's so special about leather manufacturing,

33
00:02:02,800 --> 00:02:05,460
because first of all, we're dealing with an organic material.

34
00:02:05,660 --> 00:02:09,320
We're dealing with a substrate that has come from a live animal.

35
00:02:11,540 --> 00:02:15,820
In the United States, cattle are the most common source of those animal skins,

36
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:19,020
while other countries seek out their own native sources,

37
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:21,100
including many exotic species.

38
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:24,400
And just as in prehistoric times,

39
00:02:24,401 --> 00:02:28,200
Nearly all of these creatures are primary sources of food and meat.

40
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:31,060
No part of that animal is wasted.

41
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:34,200
The skin goes one place, the meat goes another.

42
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:37,280
In that way, I regard it as respectful.

43
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:40,300
But no matter where you are in the world,

44
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:43,200
the basics of transforming animal's skin into leather

45
00:02:43,201 --> 00:02:44,440
remain the same.

46
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:47,540
Strip away all that's not needed from the hides,

47
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:50,900
then introduce natural or man-made acids

48
00:02:50,901 --> 00:02:54,100
to lock in what remains most useful and durable.

49
00:02:54,400 --> 00:03:00,900
Basically, you're impregnating the skin fiber with tannic acid,

50
00:03:00,901 --> 00:03:03,900
which builds bridges, small chemical bridges,

51
00:03:03,901 --> 00:03:06,400
between the actual fibers of the skin,

52
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:10,900
and sort of locks them open into a felted network.

53
00:03:10,901 --> 00:03:16,400
That process is known as tanning.

54
00:03:16,401 --> 00:03:20,400
And to see it work, you must travel inside the animal hide

55
00:03:20,401 --> 00:03:22,900
to the three microscopic skin layers within.

56
00:03:22,901 --> 00:03:29,900
During the tanning process, the top and bottom layers are removed, leaving only the middle layer, known as derma.

57
00:03:29,901 --> 00:03:37,900
When the tanning acids are applied, they bond with the derma's many protein fibers, creating a complex, well-lubricated hole.

58
00:03:37,901 --> 00:03:49,900
Prime tanning has been manufacturing cow and steer leather for more than 70 years, making it one of the largest leather suppliers in the country.

59
00:03:49,901 --> 00:04:02,280
Our plant capacity is 3 million pounds a week, and that converts, depending on the season, between 50 and 55,000 hides a week.

60
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:08,433
Throughout the day, a steady stream of trucks delivers hides to Prime's tannery in St.

61
00:04:08,434 --> 00:04:09,440
Joseph, Missouri.

62
00:04:10,840 --> 00:04:17,520
Each truck delivers nearly 1,000 hides, weighing more than 33 tons from nearby meatpacking facilities.

63
00:04:19,900 --> 00:04:22,580
We see a lot of parasitic damage, a lot of scratches.

64
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:25,300
We see large brands from the ranchers.

65
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:27,880
And to be able to take all those skins down the same path,

66
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,220
so you'll be able to get a finished piece of leather

67
00:04:30,221 --> 00:04:33,780
that has the same color, the same feel, the same texture,

68
00:04:34,220 --> 00:04:35,760
is such a tremendous challenge.

69
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,280
19 individual operations are required

70
00:04:39,281 --> 00:04:42,920
to transform the raw animal hides into a side of usable leather.

71
00:04:44,500 --> 00:04:47,240
Because each step directly affects the one that follows,

72
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:49,680
the entire process must be closely monitored.

73
00:04:49,900 --> 00:04:54,160
Manufacturing to where we are, the consistency is really what you want.

74
00:04:55,240 --> 00:04:58,160
It's not just, you know, take it here it is.

75
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:01,860
It's beautiful, but it has to have the right weight, the right chrome content,

76
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:06,020
the right, all the materials that make it go into a good piece of leather.

77
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:13,020
To begin, hides are loaded into a liming vessel, where they are clean to remove blood, dirt, and salt.

78
00:05:13,021 --> 00:05:19,880
Calcium hydroxide, better known as live,

79
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:23,540
is then added to remove the upper layer of skin and animal hair

80
00:05:23,541 --> 00:05:25,620
without affecting the rich derma below.

81
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:31,340
Next, the leather maker oversees a series of operations

82
00:05:31,341 --> 00:05:33,460
that will open up the fibers within the derma,

83
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:36,520
making them more receptive to the tanning agent to come.

84
00:05:38,560 --> 00:05:41,780
That includes deliming the bath with ammonium sulfate,

85
00:05:41,781 --> 00:05:44,620
as well as baiting it using a natural enzyme.

86
00:05:45,540 --> 00:05:49,580
The baiting process is a process after D-Lyme that we add an enzyme

87
00:05:49,581 --> 00:05:51,980
would come from the pancreas of an animal,

88
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:55,180
and it begins to soften the hide

89
00:05:55,181 --> 00:05:57,040
and to clean the surface of the hide.

90
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:01,440
Now the hides are ready to enter the tanning process.

91
00:06:03,020 --> 00:06:05,200
Because of its speed and controllability,

92
00:06:05,800 --> 00:06:09,740
commercial tanners, like Prime, employ chrome salts as their main tanning agent.

93
00:06:09,741 --> 00:06:14,740
These salts are introduced as the hides soak can tumble in the tanning drum.

94
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:19,640
Chrome tanning, even though it was really discovered in the late 1800s,

95
00:06:19,940 --> 00:06:21,960
it didn't really be coming to use until the 30s.

96
00:06:22,420 --> 00:06:25,940
And that's when our grandfather started really producing it in a large way.

97
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:31,300
Each tannery has its own unique formula to make the chrome agent,

98
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:33,840
developed over decades of experimentation.

99
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:39,720
Chromium salts, sulfuric acid, and other proportion.

100
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:43,740
proprietary elements are used in recipes that are highly prized trade secrets.

101
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:54,740
We have our own formula of which we think is unique that is evolved over, you know, over many, many, many, many years of fine-tuning and adjustments.

102
00:06:56,740 --> 00:07:04,740
The chrome deposits itself within the microscopic fibers of the skin, forming a water-resistant, bacteria-resistant bond.

103
00:07:06,740 --> 00:07:09,740
After 22 hours in the tanning drum, the chrome has been absorbed.

104
00:07:09,741 --> 00:07:10,620
into the hides.

105
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:13,640
As a result, their color has changed

106
00:07:13,641 --> 00:07:15,780
into what is commonly called wet blue.

107
00:07:19,240 --> 00:07:21,500
The 30,000 hides are immediately

108
00:07:21,501 --> 00:07:22,980
wrong for excess moisture.

109
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:25,560
They then undergo a series of steps

110
00:07:25,561 --> 00:07:27,200
that prepare them for their end use.

111
00:07:28,540 --> 00:07:31,020
It's pretty key that we understand our customers' process

112
00:07:31,021 --> 00:07:32,620
so that we can design our product

113
00:07:32,621 --> 00:07:34,840
to best fit their process.

114
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,360
The first step is called splitting,

115
00:07:38,361 --> 00:07:42,640
whereby the top outer portion of the hide, which features the leather's grain,

116
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:47,220
is separated from the hide's soft, fleshy underside, which has no grain at all.

117
00:07:48,060 --> 00:07:50,680
Each has its own commercial appeal and end uses.

118
00:07:51,320 --> 00:07:53,780
The under portion that is split off is called a split,

119
00:07:54,280 --> 00:07:56,780
and that goes into products such a suede leather.

120
00:07:58,580 --> 00:08:02,040
Alternatively, the top grain is featured in most leather products.

121
00:08:02,940 --> 00:08:05,500
But in order for manufacturers to work with it,

122
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:07,960
prime must guarantee a uniform thickness.

123
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:12,060
We were trying to control the operation within a tenth of a millimeter.

124
00:08:12,900 --> 00:08:21,560
Now, we have certain variability, so I would suggest that the accuracy of the machine would be plus or minus two-tenths.

125
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:30,580
After a final inspection, 80% of Primes' wet blue is sent abroad to international leathermakers.

126
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:38,340
The remaining 20%, about 9,000 hides, are transferred to Primes' Ophithe.

127
00:08:38,360 --> 00:08:43,500
tannery in Berwick, Maine, where tanners oversee the final stages of leather creation.

128
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:51,580
This is when we move into what I consider really the beginning of the actual production of leather.

129
00:08:51,780 --> 00:08:53,860
This is where it really begins to get fun.

130
00:08:56,340 --> 00:09:04,260
Finishing is the name for the collective processes that turn wet blue into the rich, colorful, and aromatic leathers with which we are all familiar.

131
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:08,340
When it arrives in Maine from Missouri, the wet color.

132
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:13,100
blue was again placed in a tanning drum, where it undergoes three final wet processes.

133
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:17,140
The total time required is no more than six hours.

134
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:24,380
During the first step, called retaning, a second set of tanning agents is introduced to the leather.

135
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:32,520
Depending on the leather's end use, these agents can be derived from vegetables, man-made sintans, or mineral agents,

136
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:36,780
all of which impart specific qualities of feel and uniformity to the leather.

137
00:09:36,781 --> 00:09:39,760
To enhance the natural beauty of leather,

138
00:09:40,360 --> 00:09:42,740
aniline-type dyes are then introduced to the bath.

139
00:09:43,740 --> 00:09:46,440
These dyes, which are derived primarily from petroleum,

140
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:48,740
come in a wide variety of colors

141
00:09:48,741 --> 00:09:52,360
and form an insoluble compound once they enter the leather's fibers.

142
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:56,040
How fast or slow these dyes are introduced

143
00:09:56,041 --> 00:09:58,040
will greatly affect the leather's color.

144
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,980
The final step is fat licking,

145
00:10:02,420 --> 00:10:04,840
which introduces certain oils into the leather.

146
00:10:04,841 --> 00:10:08,000
Leather will form around you and it has a memory.

147
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:14,840
And by providing the oils, we're protecting the collagen fiber structure within a skin, within a hide.

148
00:10:15,340 --> 00:10:20,280
And as we do subsequent operations to it, drying, conditioning, and the finishing operations,

149
00:10:20,840 --> 00:10:27,440
those oils continue to act as a barrier so the leather can remain and have its breathability and its strength characteristics.

150
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:33,320
Once these final wet processes are completed, the new leather hides must be dried.

151
00:10:33,321 --> 00:10:41,060
We want to be able to take it through the drying process, but not dry out all the natural waters and all the oils that we put in.

152
00:10:41,220 --> 00:10:45,920
So controlled drying is one of the more critical, mechanical steps in our operations.

153
00:10:47,460 --> 00:10:52,440
Crime is one of the few tanneries in the country to offer all three of the different drying techniques.

154
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:54,980
Techniques that have evolved over decades.

155
00:10:56,480 --> 00:10:58,040
The first is toggling.

156
00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:01,640
Reminiscant of ancient drying methods, it stretches the leather.

157
00:11:01,641 --> 00:11:06,300
A toggling team will connect more than 50,000 toggles a week

158
00:11:06,301 --> 00:11:08,400
during the constant flow of leather hides.

159
00:11:10,680 --> 00:11:14,820
The second is pasting, which yields the most square footage per hide.

160
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:18,600
Here the side is literally pasted to a glass plate,

161
00:11:19,060 --> 00:11:22,320
then sent into an oven where strict temperature controls are maintained.

162
00:11:25,540 --> 00:11:28,720
The third and final drying option is vacuum drying,

163
00:11:28,721 --> 00:11:30,340
the fastest of the three.

164
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:35,740
Individual wet hides are placed between two heated stainless steel plates,

165
00:11:36,240 --> 00:11:38,760
which vacuum dry the leather in three to eight minutes.

166
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:43,080
Each drying method has its own advantage.

167
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:47,180
If we're looking to have a leather that has minimal oil content

168
00:11:47,181 --> 00:11:49,960
and has a very tight grain structure,

169
00:11:50,400 --> 00:11:53,080
then those leathers tend to go through the vacuum drying process.

170
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:56,460
If we're looking for leathers that tend to go into very rugged,

171
00:11:56,640 --> 00:11:58,160
outdoor type of footwear,

172
00:11:58,620 --> 00:12:00,920
then those leathers go through our pasting process.

173
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:05,980
If we're looking for leathers that have a very soft and supple feel for it,

174
00:12:06,100 --> 00:12:09,220
whether it's athletic footwear or furniture upholstery,

175
00:12:09,700 --> 00:12:14,020
then those leathers tend to have more oils and fat liquors associated with their fiber structures.

176
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:16,480
So those will go through our toggling operations,

177
00:12:16,600 --> 00:12:18,740
which provides much more stretch into the leather.

178
00:12:20,120 --> 00:12:23,960
Once dry, the grain side undergoes a final series of processes

179
00:12:23,961 --> 00:12:26,600
during which it is mechanically stressed, buffed,

180
00:12:26,601 --> 00:12:29,520
and then coated with abrasion and stain-resistant materials.

181
00:12:29,521 --> 00:12:32,500
altogether it has taken less than a week

182
00:12:32,501 --> 00:12:34,240
to make a fine piece of leather

183
00:12:34,241 --> 00:12:38,280
at each of the 19 individual steps

184
00:12:38,281 --> 00:12:40,900
the tanner makes his indelible mark on the hides

185
00:12:40,901 --> 00:12:43,360
creating the leather which can now be fashioned

186
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:45,840
into the products that make up our daily lives

187
00:12:45,841 --> 00:12:50,940
it's someone who has a great technical knowledge

188
00:12:50,941 --> 00:12:52,260
and can do the chemistry

189
00:12:52,261 --> 00:12:55,500
but it also has to have a style and a feel for leather

190
00:12:55,501 --> 00:12:58,440
because you can have a piece of leather that by rights

191
00:12:58,441 --> 00:12:59,500
is made properly

192
00:12:59,520 --> 00:13:00,920
but just doesn't look right.

193
00:13:01,560 --> 00:13:02,860
It's a little too plasticy looking.

194
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:03,740
It's a little too bright.

195
00:13:03,860 --> 00:13:05,600
Maybe the brakes just not quite right.

196
00:13:06,200 --> 00:13:07,920
And that's all learned.

197
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:09,460
You have to love making leather.

198
00:13:11,340 --> 00:13:13,800
But even with 21st century know-how,

199
00:13:14,600 --> 00:13:16,580
some people are so passionate about leather,

200
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:19,700
but they still make it the old-fashioned way.

201
00:13:20,740 --> 00:13:23,260
In fact, they can get downright prehistoric.

202
00:13:26,960 --> 00:13:28,280
Leather is so durable

203
00:13:28,281 --> 00:13:29,280
that when discovered,

204
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:35,520
the wreck of the Titanic, leather satchels, purses, and shoes were still fully intact.

205
00:13:35,521 --> 00:13:41,520
Leather will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

206
00:13:59,520 --> 00:14:08,520
Such as chrome salts and tanning drums have propelled the science of leather making forward.

207
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:15,820
Yet for some, the old ways will always remain the best.

208
00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:21,500
There are some technologies that have not been replaced by modern technologies

209
00:14:21,501 --> 00:14:23,400
because we can't surpass them in any way.

210
00:14:23,480 --> 00:14:25,240
It's really fun when you run into something like that.

211
00:14:25,640 --> 00:14:28,240
I personally think buckskin is one of them, brain tan buckskin.

212
00:14:28,241 --> 00:14:35,980
There's no other leather that we make by modern processes that comes close to it in its suppleness, in its feel, and in its durability.

213
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:47,780
Husband and wife team Tamara Wilder and Stephen Edholm have spent 17 years mastering the Native American technique of brain tanning as part of their larger study of

214
00:14:47,781 --> 00:14:48,680
paleo-technics.

215
00:14:50,800 --> 00:14:53,554
It literally means ancient arts and technologies.

216
00:14:53,555 --> 00:14:58,220
Paleo is ancient and techniques is arts and technologies. It's not just technology.

217
00:14:58,240 --> 00:15:01,240
but the artistic side of technologies as well.

218
00:15:01,241 --> 00:15:05,240
At their camp in the hills of Northern California,

219
00:15:05,241 --> 00:15:09,240
Wilder and Edholm approach brain tanning using methods and tool designs

220
00:15:09,241 --> 00:15:12,240
that date back to the first modern humans,

221
00:15:12,241 --> 00:15:15,240
beginning with the arduous task of fleshing the hide.

222
00:15:15,241 --> 00:15:19,240
This is a traditional tanning knife.

223
00:15:19,241 --> 00:15:27,240
So what you're doing is you're just bulldozing off all of this flesh and fat.

224
00:15:27,241 --> 00:15:32,960
and I'm not going to cut the hide because I have a smooth beam surface

225
00:15:32,961 --> 00:15:38,800
and I'm just pushing all this off see all this fat and meat that's coming off

226
00:15:38,801 --> 00:15:45,400
although metal tools work faster ancient bone tools can do the job just as well

227
00:15:45,401 --> 00:15:51,300
if I wanted to I could be fleshing this with a bone tool a traditional old tool

228
00:15:51,301 --> 00:15:57,000
which has probably been used in excess of 12,000 years around the world and it's made out of a

229
00:15:57,240 --> 00:16:01,240
cannon bone of an elk and it's called a toothed flesher.

230
00:16:01,241 --> 00:16:06,240
And the way this works is it's got a really fancy handhold,

231
00:16:06,241 --> 00:16:09,240
which is incredibly brilliant, we think.

232
00:16:09,241 --> 00:16:13,240
You twist this and this wraps against your wrist

233
00:16:13,241 --> 00:16:16,240
and it gives your wrist support.

234
00:16:16,241 --> 00:16:20,240
And then I hit into the skin this way.

235
00:16:20,241 --> 00:16:22,240
And the teeth

236
00:16:22,241 --> 00:16:26,240
grab the flesh,

237
00:16:26,241 --> 00:16:29,840
grab the flesh and peel it off.

238
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:38,040
In under an hour, the deer hide is fleshed and ready to be degrained.

239
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:44,840
The sturdy grain within the skin just beneath the hair is removed,

240
00:16:45,540 --> 00:16:49,740
exposing an underlying layer that will make the buckskin soft and pliable.

241
00:16:51,840 --> 00:16:55,840
So I'm not just removing the hair, I'm removing this layer of skin.

242
00:16:56,240 --> 00:16:57,700
See, that's what I'm removing there.

243
00:16:58,820 --> 00:17:01,960
Once the hide is fleshed and degrained, it's left to dry.

244
00:17:02,660 --> 00:17:06,420
So there's the finished scraped hide.

245
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:14,420
So now the next step is to dry the hide out.

246
00:17:14,680 --> 00:17:17,800
So I drape it over a line or I can stake it out to dry it out flat.

247
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:22,520
And the reason I dry it is then when I soak it up to do the braining process,

248
00:17:23,120 --> 00:17:25,920
this membrane layer, which is on this side, which you can't see,

249
00:17:26,240 --> 00:17:30,080
will release and I can remove it and then we go with the softening process.

250
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:36,920
Surprisingly, drying makes the skin more accepting of the brain-based softening agents to follow.

251
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:40,920
Many hides are dried from six months to a year.

252
00:17:43,800 --> 00:17:46,900
Once the drying is finished, the brain tanning can begin.

253
00:17:47,740 --> 00:17:52,400
The brains are cooked to destroy pathogens and then mashed into a fine solution.

254
00:17:53,280 --> 00:17:56,080
Other things that have been used historically are

255
00:17:56,240 --> 00:18:03,000
spinal fluid, which has the same chemicals as in the brain, the spinal fluid of eels,

256
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,960
things like that, fish eggs.

257
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:19,840
For the brains to fully penetrate the hide, it's crucial to stretch and open the fibers.

258
00:18:23,040 --> 00:18:26,220
Once the brain solution is softened the hide, the emerging leather is,

259
00:18:26,240 --> 00:18:28,000
again scraped and bathed.

260
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:34,080
When we soak the hide in the brains,

261
00:18:34,480 --> 00:18:37,260
the fatty acids, when the brains penetrate the skin

262
00:18:37,261 --> 00:18:40,560
and coat these fibers and lubricate them

263
00:18:40,561 --> 00:18:43,160
and allow us to manually soften the skin,

264
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:44,980
moving it all the time,

265
00:18:45,600 --> 00:18:49,400
to keep the fibers stretched and fluffed apart.

266
00:18:51,940 --> 00:18:55,340
After that, we'll smoke it over a slow fire

267
00:18:55,341 --> 00:18:58,340
and the smoke penetrates the skin

268
00:18:58,341 --> 00:19:00,940
and forms small chemical bonds

269
00:19:00,941 --> 00:19:02,860
between the fibers of the skin,

270
00:19:03,020 --> 00:19:04,040
locking them open.

271
00:19:07,820 --> 00:19:10,060
Stephen builds a fire in a deep pit

272
00:19:10,061 --> 00:19:11,920
using tree bark as kindling.

273
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:18,620
The hides are attached to a skirt

274
00:19:18,621 --> 00:19:20,980
that creates a funnel for the dense smoke.

275
00:19:21,420 --> 00:19:22,500
This is what we like to see,

276
00:19:22,580 --> 00:19:23,920
this billowing smoke.

277
00:19:25,340 --> 00:19:32,340
Within 20 minutes, the hide takes on the distinctive color and texture of a fine piece of leather.

278
00:19:32,341 --> 00:19:39,340
Now I'm going to untie this and then reach in and turn it inside out.

279
00:19:39,341 --> 00:19:43,340
And this is the magic when you get to see the smoke color.

280
00:19:43,341 --> 00:19:47,340
Come out, starts to look like buckskin.

281
00:19:47,341 --> 00:19:51,340
Once a hide has been smoked, it's officially leather.

282
00:19:51,341 --> 00:19:54,340
When complete, the smoked hide is durable, washable, and washable.

283
00:19:54,341 --> 00:19:58,340
washable and warm, and a small piece of living history.

284
00:19:58,341 --> 00:20:02,340
The tactile experience of doing these processes,

285
00:20:02,341 --> 00:20:04,340
it wakes something up in your brain.

286
00:20:04,341 --> 00:20:07,340
This is every human's common ancestry.

287
00:20:07,341 --> 00:20:10,340
We're doing stone age living skills,

288
00:20:10,341 --> 00:20:12,340
and every human alive on the earth today

289
00:20:12,341 --> 00:20:15,340
has ancestors back in their history

290
00:20:15,341 --> 00:20:18,340
who did these skills and who were good at them.

291
00:20:18,341 --> 00:20:22,340
No one knows exactly when Tanning was first discovered.

292
00:20:22,341 --> 00:20:27,040
Greek warriors in the 13th century BC protected themselves with leather armor,

293
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:28,980
as Homer notes in the Iliad.

294
00:20:30,280 --> 00:20:36,020
And from Gaul to Jerusalem, Rome's armies wore leather-sold shoes and carried leather shields.

295
00:20:37,120 --> 00:20:41,580
In fact, leather shields would remain commonplace in many armies well into the 18th century.

296
00:20:43,620 --> 00:20:47,700
During the Middle Ages, Muslim nations became leading producers of fine leather,

297
00:20:48,340 --> 00:20:52,320
especially Moroccan goat skin, which is still highly prized today.

298
00:20:52,340 --> 00:20:56,420
The goatskin was placed in large vats

299
00:20:56,421 --> 00:21:00,880
and soaked with fruit and vegetable extracts, rich in natural tannins.

300
00:21:01,840 --> 00:21:06,320
Those tannins could produce greater varieties of leather than brain or other animal matter,

301
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,900
even as the process took nearly nine months to complete.

302
00:21:11,100 --> 00:21:16,620
By the 12th century, these ancient alchemies have been codified into a more exacting science.

303
00:21:18,340 --> 00:21:22,320
In the 12th century in the UK in particular and also throughout Europe,

304
00:21:22,340 --> 00:21:24,640
guilds had been established

305
00:21:24,641 --> 00:21:27,460
to control the actual process in the weather

306
00:21:27,461 --> 00:21:31,280
and to install a degree of consistency and quality.

307
00:21:32,080 --> 00:21:34,500
For the next 700 years,

308
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:37,900
the science and art of tanning would change only incrementally.

309
00:21:38,280 --> 00:21:39,980
Until the 19th century,

310
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:43,520
when American leather makers embraced new frontiers

311
00:21:43,521 --> 00:21:46,000
and furthered an industrial revolution.

312
00:21:50,600 --> 00:21:52,320
Choices for the leather buying.

313
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:59,340
in ancient Rome included leather made from jackals, hyenas, wolves, bears, and even leopards.

314
00:21:59,341 --> 00:22:04,340
Leather will return on modern marvels.

315
00:22:22,340 --> 00:22:36,340
Ask any rodeo cowboy, and he'll tell you that in the competition between man and beast, only the toughest survive.

316
00:22:42,880 --> 00:22:47,460
And since the days of the Old West, the toughest cowboys have always worn leather.

317
00:22:47,461 --> 00:22:53,920
at Muir and McDonald, one of America's oldest tanneries,

318
00:22:54,380 --> 00:22:56,820
they still make leather just as their forefathers did

319
00:22:56,821 --> 00:22:59,140
when the company opened in 1863.

320
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:04,800
In fact, no new piece of machinery has been purchased here since 1937,

321
00:23:05,060 --> 00:23:08,760
and the factory retains its antique flavor and natural light.

322
00:23:09,380 --> 00:23:11,580
Now run by the family's fourth generation,

323
00:23:12,200 --> 00:23:15,460
Muir and McDonald's primary product has been the same for decades.

324
00:23:15,461 --> 00:23:19,460
Steer leather, the ideal material for saddle makers.

325
00:23:19,461 --> 00:23:27,460
Our production is 60 hides a week, which is pretty small compared to some of the other tanners.

326
00:23:27,461 --> 00:23:35,460
Generally though, we're looking for a good, full-growing native steer, and we like the hides to weigh at least 70 pounds apiece.

327
00:23:35,461 --> 00:23:44,460
Unlike today's large-scale commercial tanneries, Muir and McDonald's creates their leather using an historic method known as vegetable tanning.

328
00:23:44,461 --> 00:23:46,620
as vegetable tanning.

329
00:23:46,621 --> 00:23:48,240
Rather than using chrome salts,

330
00:23:48,241 --> 00:23:51,160
vegetable tanners rely on natural chemical compounds

331
00:23:51,161 --> 00:23:55,800
called tannins, a word derived from the Celtic or oak.

332
00:23:55,801 --> 00:23:57,880
Tannins can be found in vegetables, fruits,

333
00:23:57,881 --> 00:23:59,760
and especially tree bark, which

334
00:23:59,761 --> 00:24:03,300
has particularly high concentrations.

335
00:24:03,301 --> 00:24:04,900
Because they're based in Oregon,

336
00:24:04,901 --> 00:24:08,040
Muir and McDonald relies on the bark of the local Douglas fir

337
00:24:08,041 --> 00:24:09,780
for their tanning extract.

338
00:24:09,781 --> 00:24:13,060
We grind 1,400 pounds of bark twice a week.

339
00:24:13,061 --> 00:24:14,260
We have large wooden vexed.

340
00:24:14,261 --> 00:24:20,160
vats that we cook the bark in and basically it's like percolating coffee is about as close as I can get to

341
00:24:20,161 --> 00:24:22,060
explain how it works.

342
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:27,600
Vegetable tanning is not for the impatient or careless.

343
00:24:28,940 --> 00:24:35,360
After hair removal and prep, the tanning process requires 10 weeks to complete, during which time,

344
00:24:35,520 --> 00:24:38,160
steadily increasing levels of bark tannins are applied.

345
00:24:38,161 --> 00:24:44,860
hides are draped over a rope inside the tanning vessel

346
00:24:44,861 --> 00:24:48,840
and kept in constant motion for 12 to 14 hours a day

347
00:24:48,841 --> 00:24:51,020
and a procedure known as rocking.

348
00:24:52,560 --> 00:24:54,640
This motion keeps the tannins stirred

349
00:24:54,641 --> 00:24:56,800
and opens the hide to their absorption.

350
00:24:57,360 --> 00:24:58,060
That looks good.

351
00:24:59,800 --> 00:25:01,760
After 10 weeks in the tanning pits,

352
00:25:01,761 --> 00:25:04,720
the hides are placed inside a stuffing wheel

353
00:25:04,721 --> 00:25:07,580
where oils lost during tanning are replaced.

354
00:25:08,160 --> 00:25:14,420
The resulting leather is handsome, sturdy, and ready to be carved, tooled, and embossed by a saddlemaker.

355
00:25:17,120 --> 00:25:26,160
In 1980, Muir and MacDonald was designated an historic national landmark, an apt tribute to nearly 150 years of American craftsmanship.

356
00:25:27,600 --> 00:25:35,878
And I can't explain why I've been here for 22 years except that I get a real enjoyment out of taking something that's a raw piece of material like a cow-eyed and

357
00:25:35,879 --> 00:25:38,140
taking them all the way through the process.

358
00:25:38,160 --> 00:25:40,400
and have a beautiful piece of leather at the end.

359
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:44,840
So I think once it's in the blood, I think it's there to stay.

360
00:25:46,100 --> 00:25:49,880
In fact, leather making has been in America's blood since a pilgrim named

361
00:25:49,881 --> 00:25:54,440
Fillerman Dickerson began making leather almost upon his arrival in 1620.

362
00:25:56,940 --> 00:26:02,940
And as America expanded westward, pioneers adopted ancient techniques from the Native Americans they encountered.

363
00:26:02,941 --> 00:26:09,140
Leather made in factories like Muir and McDonald's,

364
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:12,520
traversed the Oregon Trail, fought in the Indian Wars,

365
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:16,380
and pursued commodities like gold and furs across the West.

366
00:26:18,080 --> 00:26:21,200
Later, celebrity cowboys like Buffalo Bill Cody

367
00:26:21,201 --> 00:26:23,700
made leather clothing their signature style.

368
00:26:25,580 --> 00:26:28,980
But the Industrial Revolution created new demands for leather

369
00:26:28,981 --> 00:26:31,840
and gave rise to new innovations in leather making.

370
00:26:31,841 --> 00:26:35,860
Nowhere was that more true than in the Northeast Corridor,

371
00:26:36,340 --> 00:26:38,660
once the proud center of America's leather industry.

372
00:26:40,540 --> 00:26:43,200
Of all the towns that symbolize American leather,

373
00:26:43,800 --> 00:26:46,360
none was more prominent than Peabody, Massachusetts.

374
00:26:47,700 --> 00:26:53,240
Peabody was the leather capital of the world from about 1900 to 1950.

375
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:55,940
They made more leather in Peabody,

376
00:26:55,941 --> 00:27:01,820
a town of 15, 20,000 people than any place else in them.

377
00:27:01,840 --> 00:27:02,840
the world.

378
00:27:02,841 --> 00:27:09,320
Because of Peabody's unique confluence of rivers and streams, it was an ideal location

379
00:27:09,321 --> 00:27:13,200
for the water-reliant process.

380
00:27:13,201 --> 00:27:18,260
The tanners were a very inventive group.

381
00:27:18,261 --> 00:27:27,680
They were entrepreneurs, they were gamblers, they were men that were going to make money,

382
00:27:27,681 --> 00:27:31,220
and they had a lot of, lot of power.

383
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:38,840
Under their direction, the American leather-making industry became an international giant.

384
00:27:38,841 --> 00:27:49,086
The first chrome tanning process was developed in 1884, and the introduction of the tanning drop greatly increased the number of hides that could be processed in a

385
00:27:49,087 --> 00:27:49,840
single wash.

386
00:27:49,841 --> 00:27:59,840
By 1940, Peabody featured more than 50 major tanneries and related industries, and processed more than 27 million hides annually.

387
00:27:59,841 --> 00:28:04,840
But less than a century later, no tanneries remained.

388
00:28:04,841 --> 00:28:10,840
The depression, consolidation, and especially international competition,

389
00:28:10,841 --> 00:28:15,840
all contributed to the decline of peabody and the American leather industry.

390
00:28:15,841 --> 00:28:19,840
The whole thing, it just grew, it flowered,

391
00:28:19,841 --> 00:28:24,840
and there were thousands and thousands of people that had a good life here,

392
00:28:24,841 --> 00:28:26,840
but it passed its day.

393
00:28:26,841 --> 00:28:37,880
The final straw came when environmental regulation focused on the industry's decades-old practice of chemical dumping and its consequent pollution.

394
00:28:39,000 --> 00:28:43,600
Historically, the tannin industry has not been a particularly good neighbor.

395
00:28:44,600 --> 00:28:50,100
Going back to ancient times, they used to ban the tanner from the town and make him live outside the town wall.

396
00:28:52,280 --> 00:28:56,220
Biosolids from animal flesh, chrome salts, dyes for finishing.

397
00:28:56,840 --> 00:29:01,100
All are essential tools of the tanner, but they're also toxic pollutants,

398
00:29:01,600 --> 00:29:04,420
which for too long were flushed daily into local waterways.

399
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:12,000
Austin Harbor was one of the most polluted places in the country.

400
00:29:12,940 --> 00:29:18,780
Salem Harbor was also polluted from the runoffs of the tanneries.

401
00:29:20,680 --> 00:29:25,700
Today, American tanneries, such as prime, are at the forefront of environmental efforts

402
00:29:25,701 --> 00:29:26,860
within the leather industry.

403
00:29:28,460 --> 00:29:32,720
On average, Prime uses more than 1 million gallons of water every day,

404
00:29:33,300 --> 00:29:36,040
and most of it will blend with chromium, a pollutant.

405
00:29:38,140 --> 00:29:46,600
All of the chrome effluence and wastewaters are diverted into a chrome recovery system.

406
00:29:47,600 --> 00:29:54,580
And ultimately, 20% of the chrome that we use in our tanning process comes from our

407
00:29:54,581 --> 00:29:56,780
a chrome recycling plant.

408
00:29:58,180 --> 00:30:01,980
All water leaving prime must meet strict environmental codes.

409
00:30:04,440 --> 00:30:07,440
Sulfites and biosolids are removed both through chemicals

410
00:30:07,441 --> 00:30:11,620
and a biological process that occurs in a 3 million gallon

411
00:30:11,621 --> 00:30:12,660
airation lagoon.

412
00:30:16,260 --> 00:30:19,960
From there, the water is diverted to a local treatment facility for further

413
00:30:19,961 --> 00:30:20,661
purification.

414
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:24,560
From all these biosolids that we jacked,

415
00:30:24,580 --> 00:30:28,080
we add a chemical or a polymer to help remove the water.

416
00:30:28,081 --> 00:30:32,580
Now this solid material is transported to farms

417
00:30:32,581 --> 00:30:35,580
around the St. Joseph Community Area

418
00:30:35,581 --> 00:30:39,580
and we generate basically a hundred tons a day,

419
00:30:39,581 --> 00:30:44,580
3,000 tons a month, which is added, actually,

420
00:30:44,581 --> 00:30:49,580
is registered as a fertilizer, and we provide at this time free of charge

421
00:30:49,581 --> 00:30:51,580
to farmers.

422
00:30:51,581 --> 00:30:53,580
Over the course of a year, that's enough fertilizers

423
00:30:53,581 --> 00:30:58,420
That's enough fertilizer to blanket 2,500 acres of Midwest farmland.

424
00:31:00,080 --> 00:31:03,680
But cowhide is just one kind of American leather in demand.

425
00:31:04,580 --> 00:31:07,640
In fact, many leathers can get downright exotic.

426
00:31:11,700 --> 00:31:14,260
Peabody wasn't the only leather center in New England.

427
00:31:15,160 --> 00:31:17,020
By the first decade of the 20th century,

428
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:21,700
more than 50,000 buggy whips a week were made in Westfield, Massachusetts,

429
00:31:21,701 --> 00:31:24,460
earning it the title Whip City.

430
00:31:25,840 --> 00:31:28,260
Leather will return on Modern Marvels.

431
00:31:51,700 --> 00:31:52,408
warriors.

432
00:31:52,409 --> 00:31:58,700
To each, a sword was not only the source of power, but an extension of the soul.

433
00:31:58,701 --> 00:32:05,700
As such, the blade was honed of the finest steel, and the grip made of chagrin,

434
00:32:05,701 --> 00:32:07,860
a leather derived from the stingray.

435
00:32:07,861 --> 00:32:11,700
It's so durable that it assured the samurai's grasp of his sword

436
00:32:11,701 --> 00:32:15,700
even when covered in his enemy's blood.

437
00:32:15,701 --> 00:32:20,700
It was Marco Polo who in the 13th century first traded his own letters for the rare and mysterious species.

438
00:32:20,701 --> 00:32:22,540
and mysterious species of the east.

439
00:32:24,600 --> 00:32:27,840
And since that time, our fascination for exotic

440
00:32:27,841 --> 00:32:29,580
leathers has only intensified.

441
00:32:30,700 --> 00:32:33,480
The exotics are, for the most part, more primitive animals.

442
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:36,720
Many of them are unchanged in the fossil record.

443
00:32:37,380 --> 00:32:40,520
And as such, they are armored animals.

444
00:32:40,560 --> 00:32:41,620
They were built for war.

445
00:32:43,800 --> 00:32:46,260
Gliding underwater, a sting-raised back

446
00:32:46,261 --> 00:32:47,660
appears light and smooth.

447
00:32:48,260 --> 00:32:50,680
But when dried, it forms small, nearly

448
00:32:50,700 --> 00:32:52,660
ceramic beads.

449
00:32:52,661 --> 00:32:56,880
The stingray skin is composed of these little hard beads

450
00:32:56,881 --> 00:32:59,760
of dentile, maybe about the equivalent of 60 or 80

451
00:32:59,761 --> 00:33:03,400
grit sandpaper, that are growing out of a true leather

452
00:33:03,401 --> 00:33:05,460
substrate.

453
00:33:05,461 --> 00:33:08,200
To transform stingray into chagreen, the tanner

454
00:33:08,201 --> 00:33:11,800
must apply agents like chrome that penetrate the armor in order

455
00:33:11,801 --> 00:33:14,920
to transform the dermal layer beneath.

456
00:33:14,921 --> 00:33:17,220
Once the leather is formed below, the tanner

457
00:33:17,221 --> 00:33:20,220
buffs the denteel beads to create the unique sheen.

458
00:33:20,221 --> 00:33:29,460
In this case, the skin was left in full bead, as you find it in sword grips for medieval times.

459
00:33:31,540 --> 00:33:37,700
However, the tanner may also choose to buff off the top of the cone-shaped beads, creating a stunning effect.

460
00:33:38,340 --> 00:33:44,500
It causes a little window into the cartilage, and it becomes this exotically speckled surface.

461
00:33:44,900 --> 00:33:48,840
It throws a great deal of light, and I find it fascinating.

462
00:33:50,220 --> 00:34:00,220
Throughout history, Chagrin's special beauty and durability have made it a favorite of eastern warriors and western emperors alike, including Napoleon.

463
00:34:03,220 --> 00:34:07,220
But Chagrin isn't the only exotic with dramatic visual appeal.

464
00:34:09,220 --> 00:34:13,220
And today, many species provide strikingly handsome leathers.

465
00:34:14,220 --> 00:34:19,220
Often this visual mystique is enhanced by the potentially deadly nature of their source.

466
00:34:19,221 --> 00:34:25,200
Here's an example with two pythons, one African, one Vietnamese.

467
00:34:26,520 --> 00:34:32,400
This is, as the snake goes through the forest, this is the pattern that you'll see.

468
00:34:32,500 --> 00:34:33,240
This is the back.

469
00:34:34,140 --> 00:34:39,420
And then the belly has been cut down the middle, and so you're seeing the larger scales on the side.

470
00:34:42,080 --> 00:34:48,120
But of all the exotics, none has proved more popular or more prized than the crocodilian.

471
00:34:49,220 --> 00:34:54,660
The gradation in the crocodilians goes from Cayman to Saltwater Croc to American Alligator.

472
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:58,540
The American Alligator is the platinum standard of the crocodilians.

473
